1
00:00:17,989 --> 00:00:22,171
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha praises seclusion in the suttas in many places.

2
00:00:23,132 --> 00:00:32,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, assuming that that is what we should be doing, being secluded, how can we get ready for that seclusion?

3
00:00:33,097 --> 00:00:39,120
[SPEAKER_00]: What can we do so that we can actually live in physical seclusion and actually benefit from it?

4
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:40,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

5
00:00:42,062 --> 00:00:42,202
[SPEAKER_00]: The

6
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:52,218
[SPEAKER_01]: The most obvious thing is basically to start exposing yourself to it, to seclusion.

7
00:00:53,419 --> 00:01:10,067
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you're not going to prepare yourself for seclusion if you never leave the group, if you never even leave the group for the periods of time, if you never spend time alone, if you're always surrounded by people.

8
00:01:10,708 --> 00:01:14,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Preparedness for seclusion cannot come out of it, because it's completely the opposite of it.

9
00:01:16,302 --> 00:01:24,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as helpful as people can be for one's well-being and so on, it's a compromise.

10
00:01:25,747 --> 00:01:27,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Needing others, it's a compromise.

11
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:31,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether, you know, as a layperson or a monk, it doesn't matter.

12
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:39,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But when I say needing others, I'm talking more specifically about needing others for your existential well-being.

13
00:01:41,082 --> 00:01:43,524
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a... that is a... that is a... is a massive risk.

14
00:01:44,185 --> 00:01:49,169
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a... that's a massive compromise, if you never, sort of, develop out of it.

15
00:01:51,211 --> 00:01:58,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, that all those reflections that we are all owners of our actions, nobody... Basically, what you do stays with you.

16
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:03,742
[SPEAKER_01]: You live... you live this life, then you go to the next one, the next one, or whatever.

17
00:02:04,162 --> 00:02:04,763
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is,

18
00:02:05,762 --> 00:02:07,563
[SPEAKER_01]: No other people can help you with that.

19
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:10,586
[SPEAKER_01]: No other people can take away your burden or add to it.

20
00:02:10,706 --> 00:02:11,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's always on you.

21
00:02:12,627 --> 00:02:16,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Your intentions, your decisions, your actions will always stay in you.

22
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:18,592
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you're bound with your actions and the results of it.

23
00:02:19,832 --> 00:02:23,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, existentially, fundamentally, you're alone whether you want to be or not.

24
00:02:24,596 --> 00:02:32,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the practice comes out of the first step of recognition of that very existential principle, that you are alone, that you cannot be.

25
00:02:33,535 --> 00:02:36,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always... the experience is always me and others.

26
00:02:37,298 --> 00:02:44,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if others are your best, closest friends and partners and relatives and family, it's still others.

27
00:02:44,343 --> 00:02:47,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have very close others, but for you, it's still just you.

28
00:02:48,886 --> 00:02:53,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when it comes to your feelings, your intentions, your responsibility, that cannot be shared among the group.

29
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:56,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Only as an excuse, basically, people would use it.

30
00:02:57,903 --> 00:03:08,790
[SPEAKER_01]: I can tell you that I feel like this and that... Yeah, yeah, but that dimension of your feeling is inherently inaccessible to me, inasmuch as I mention my feeling is inherently accessible to anybody other than me.

31
00:03:10,291 --> 00:03:19,998
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so it's it's not... it doesn't mean solitude is not optional in other talks, because existence is not optional, and existence is solitary, basically.

32
00:03:21,459 --> 00:03:25,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why if somebody wants to overcome and undo, bhava, the existence,

33
00:03:26,624 --> 00:03:29,865
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to start recognizing these inherent principles of it.

34
00:03:30,986 --> 00:03:38,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as you don't leave the group, how can you develop towards solitude?

35
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:39,789
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you see through it?

36
00:03:40,829 --> 00:03:47,512
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you discern that the neutral feeling that the Buddha praises, basically, for which solitude is required?

37
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:50,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise you're going to have...

38
00:03:53,194 --> 00:03:55,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people who are not used to solitude, when they are left alone,

39
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:59,902
[SPEAKER_01]: The boredom is the first result of their experience.

40
00:04:01,283 --> 00:04:02,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So they experience boredom.

41
00:04:04,164 --> 00:04:05,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is kind of, oh, just bored.

42
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:07,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually much more than just that.

43
00:04:07,126 --> 00:04:14,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you stay with your boredom, you start turning into this restless, panic, fear, anxiety.

44
00:04:14,630 --> 00:04:18,953
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you realize how much you actually depend on your perception of others around you.

45
00:04:20,113 --> 00:04:24,616
[SPEAKER_01]: How much you depend on that in order to basically conceal that inherent

46
00:04:25,512 --> 00:04:30,837
[SPEAKER_01]: ...nature of existence, that you are alone and fundamentally you're not in control either.

47
00:04:31,598 --> 00:04:34,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people... people are so terrified of boredom generally.

48
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:45,089
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's... it's... the whole existence, the whole lifestyle goes against that principle of existence, that you're not in your control, that you are alone.

49
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:51,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why every little reminders of that are very frightening for people who are not used to it.

50
00:04:52,417 --> 00:04:58,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, of course, you can't expect to just jump into it overnight, because, yeah, you could legitimately lose your mind.

51
00:04:59,758 --> 00:05:08,660
[SPEAKER_01]: But, at the same time, to use that as an excuse to never do it and never prepare yourself for it, well, that's equally ignorant.

52
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:20,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to recognize, okay, I'm not ready to jump into that extreme solitude right away, which means I should make myself ready.

53
00:05:20,523 --> 00:05:21,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what do you think death is?

54
00:05:23,453 --> 00:05:26,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And is it any different?

55
00:05:26,113 --> 00:05:31,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, cessation, cancellation of all of your means of escape from yourself.

56
00:05:31,854 --> 00:05:36,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Cancellation of sensuality, cancellation of company, cancellation of distraction.

57
00:05:38,056 --> 00:05:49,017
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why death is like its ultimate terror for a putrugena who is fundamentally, existentially dependent upon others, upon sensuality, upon distraction.

58
00:05:50,898 --> 00:05:52,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, using examples of

59
00:05:53,113 --> 00:05:58,155
[SPEAKER_01]: People jumping into solitude too quickly, losing their mind, as a proof that you shouldn't do that.

60
00:05:58,835 --> 00:06:02,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's just non-wisdom speaking.

61
00:06:02,717 --> 00:06:05,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Using examples to prove that you can't do it too quickly?

62
00:06:05,878 --> 00:06:06,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

63
00:06:07,239 --> 00:06:08,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means slow down a bit.

64
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:10,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But that still remains the goal.

65
00:06:11,421 --> 00:06:17,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you go to it fast or slowly, does not compromise the quality of the goal, which is developing of the solitude.

66
00:06:20,845 --> 00:06:21,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

67
00:06:21,935 --> 00:06:24,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's a lay person, or a monk, doesn't matter.

68
00:06:25,557 --> 00:06:37,922
[SPEAKER_01]: You review your mind, reflect upon, and see, am I... would I be ready to live in solitude, without having anything to do, without having any interactions, or at least for the majority of my day?

69
00:06:39,562 --> 00:06:41,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I be able to just be with myself?

70
00:06:42,243 --> 00:06:50,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would I have to, sort of, extra commit to some sort of meditation technique, to keep doing this, basically just finding things to do?

71
00:06:51,365 --> 00:06:54,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I need things to do in the solitude or would I be at ease?

72
00:06:55,707 --> 00:06:57,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever the answer... Delighting in solitude.

73
00:06:57,588 --> 00:06:57,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

74
00:06:58,108 --> 00:06:59,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Delighting in solitude.

75
00:06:59,249 --> 00:07:00,549
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm at ease in solitude.

76
00:07:00,589 --> 00:07:01,310
[SPEAKER_01]: I prefer it.

77
00:07:01,930 --> 00:07:03,551
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes work and development.

78
00:07:03,991 --> 00:07:06,372
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, I mean, don't take my word for it.

79
00:07:06,472 --> 00:07:11,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Read the suttas and just... all the time, like learning how to delight in solitude.

80
00:07:12,035 --> 00:07:15,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you can't cope with others or because you hate others.

81
00:07:15,176 --> 00:07:16,957
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are wrong reasons and they're obvious.

82
00:07:16,977 --> 00:07:19,238
[SPEAKER_01]: They're going to become obvious to whoever goes into solitude.

83
00:07:20,113 --> 00:07:24,518
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just genuinely preferring it over company, preferring it over interaction.

84
00:07:24,538 --> 00:07:26,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you're lazy or undeveloped.

85
00:07:27,321 --> 00:07:38,432
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you see it as a basis of wholesome, because you see it as a... as mirroring the principle, the basic existential principle that you're not running away from.

86
00:07:38,472 --> 00:07:42,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... through that discernment, then it will support mindfulness and everything else.

87
00:07:44,029 --> 00:07:44,570
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing.

88
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:45,971
[SPEAKER_01]: You ask yourself, am I ready for this?

89
00:07:46,111 --> 00:07:46,291
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

90
00:07:46,812 --> 00:07:47,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

91
00:07:47,172 --> 00:07:50,736
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a lay person, I should really start preparing myself for it.

92
00:07:50,796 --> 00:07:56,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I never go into complete solitude or ordain, become a monk or whatever, I will get sick.

93
00:07:56,482 --> 00:07:57,103
[SPEAKER_01]: I will die.

94
00:07:57,563 --> 00:07:58,625
[SPEAKER_01]: My senses will fail.

95
00:07:59,065 --> 00:08:01,908
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you think it feels, your senses failing?

96
00:08:01,948 --> 00:08:05,492
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels like you're basically being confined more and more and more

97
00:08:06,047 --> 00:08:08,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Into one solitary box.

98
00:08:08,649 --> 00:08:09,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Imprisoned.

99
00:08:09,590 --> 00:08:15,494
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha gave that simile of the four mountains coming closer and closer from every direction and crushing you eventually.

100
00:08:16,335 --> 00:08:18,716
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you have less and less space to move.

101
00:08:20,518 --> 00:08:28,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Less and less possibility of engaging with senses, which means less and less possibility of maintaining the domain of sensuality, the domain of distraction.

102
00:08:31,125 --> 00:08:34,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your whole life is about avoiding that,

103
00:08:35,906 --> 00:08:38,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, you think you're practicing dhamma?

104
00:08:38,607 --> 00:08:40,047
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a complete contradiction in terms.

105
00:08:41,347 --> 00:08:46,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody denies that others can be helpful, and a group of like-minded people is helpful.

106
00:08:47,008 --> 00:08:47,528
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just...

107
00:08:48,008 --> 00:08:50,889
[SPEAKER_01]: In as much, school is helpful for somebody who is learning a craft.

108
00:08:52,269 --> 00:08:53,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can't do the craft for you.

109
00:08:54,710 --> 00:08:59,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they can help you initially, if you perceive them as knowing more than you, and so on.

110
00:08:59,551 --> 00:09:00,391
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, find a...

111
00:09:01,610 --> 00:09:05,533
[SPEAKER_01]: If you find a friend that's equal or superior, yeah, go and learn from them.

112
00:09:05,793 --> 00:09:14,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like associate for somebody who you know you can't learn anything from, on the pretext of dhamma, but still, just because of the association in itself.

113
00:09:15,139 --> 00:09:22,644
[SPEAKER_01]: And that, before you or others sort of quote that sutta about the noble friendship, it's quite obvious what the Buddha meant by it.

114
00:09:23,025 --> 00:09:28,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Very often you hear that quote, that the holy friendship is the whole of the holy life.

115
00:09:29,369 --> 00:09:30,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Having a good friend, it's like

116
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:32,174
[SPEAKER_01]: The entire holy life.

117
00:09:33,294 --> 00:09:36,396
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the Buddha stopped there, then, yeah, you could say, okay, we need friends.

118
00:09:37,217 --> 00:09:40,859
[SPEAKER_01]: But then the Buddha goes in the same sutra, the next line, and he says what he meant by it.

119
00:09:40,919 --> 00:09:46,522
[SPEAKER_01]: He meant that here a young man hears about the Buddha and the possibility of enlightenment.

120
00:09:48,783 --> 00:09:52,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that, he regards him as a friend at that time.

121
00:09:53,326 --> 00:09:58,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that friendship in his mind towards such and such person who maybe became enlightened,

122
00:09:59,778 --> 00:10:05,562
[SPEAKER_01]: He develops faith on account of which he goes forward, becomes a monk, and then becomes enlightened as a result of his efforts.

123
00:10:05,762 --> 00:10:06,722
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the meaning.

124
00:10:06,963 --> 00:10:09,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly, yeah, that's the whole meaning of that.

125
00:10:09,364 --> 00:10:20,311
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you find the good friend to learn from, but not the good friend to indulge your friendship, because you're too afraid to be alone, or too distracted, or too anxious.

126
00:10:21,965 --> 00:10:23,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... really, it's not negotiable at all.

127
00:10:23,946 --> 00:10:33,152
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, whoever attained the right view in the suttas, all the Buddha would tell is that, keep... go to solitude, empty heart, root of a tree, do it, do it, because you would need that solitude for that.

128
00:10:33,572 --> 00:10:36,174
[SPEAKER_01]: And the person with the right view would... would actually do it rightly.

129
00:10:37,314 --> 00:10:39,396
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't be using solitude for any wrong reasons.

130
00:10:40,396 --> 00:10:44,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha was so... just constantly just repeating that.

131
00:10:44,619 --> 00:10:49,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And we said that before, when Ananda was with all those other arahants and āryas sewing robes for Katina, and

132
00:10:51,604 --> 00:10:58,446
[SPEAKER_01]: A band of fishermen at the market shouting, and I'm sure they were very mindful and composed, certainly for our modern standards.

133
00:10:59,026 --> 00:11:00,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the Buddha was mocking them.

134
00:11:00,467 --> 00:11:01,387
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, it's ridiculous.

135
00:11:01,527 --> 00:11:02,187
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you doing there?

136
00:11:03,628 --> 00:11:05,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, Lord, we're selling drops for a catina.

137
00:11:05,828 --> 00:11:06,889
[SPEAKER_01]: You're like a band of fishermen.

138
00:11:08,469 --> 00:11:12,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And they started talking about the solitude and seclusion and non-socializing.

139
00:11:13,171 --> 00:11:13,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

140
00:11:13,731 --> 00:11:16,912
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't expect that overnight, like, oh, I'll do it.

141
00:11:17,992 --> 00:11:21,113
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean that you don't start preparing yourself for it.

142
00:11:22,442 --> 00:11:25,565
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to develop mental strength or any form of strength.

143
00:11:26,345 --> 00:11:29,868
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize your weakness, then you recognize what you want to develop.

144
00:11:30,569 --> 00:11:40,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But avoiding solitude would be equivalent of you recognize your weakness and then justify to yourself that you shouldn't develop strength in the first place.

145
00:11:42,079 --> 00:11:43,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I can't go into solitude.

146
00:11:43,580 --> 00:11:44,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you shouldn't.

147
00:11:44,922 --> 00:11:46,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Company is the goal.

148
00:11:46,103 --> 00:11:46,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Company.

149
00:11:46,743 --> 00:11:48,665
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the Buddha said it's the noble friendship.

150
00:11:49,712 --> 00:11:50,233
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't.

151
00:11:50,653 --> 00:11:51,955
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, it's the noble friendship.

152
00:11:52,316 --> 00:11:56,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You find the friends that, at least, theoretically,

153
00:11:59,139 --> 00:12:07,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that friendship, because of that trust, you try to match that development, you try to match that enlightenment of the Buddha.

154
00:12:08,167 --> 00:12:21,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, in that sense, it was the attitude of friendship that determined your whole life as a monk, as somebody who practiced, but not the actual social... socially interacting and engaging with everyone and no one, really.

